@* ChangePassword.razor — Issue #02 fix + Issue #08 extension + UI fix
   Static-rendered "Change Password" page. Reached two ways:
     1. Forced redirect: when the user signs in with
        ApplicationUser.MustChangePassword = true, Login.razor redirects
        here, and the MustChangePasswordRedirectMiddleware in Program.cs
        blocks any other path until the password is changed.
     2. Voluntary: a signed-in user navigates here directly to change
        their own password.

   WHY STATIC SSR (not a Blazor circuit):
     Same reason as Login.razor / ResetPassword.razor — the page handles
     a form POST and re-issues the auth cookie on the HTTP response in
     the same request. A Blazor circuit would buffer the response and
     the cookie wouldn't be visible until a full reload.

   FLOW:
     1. User visits /Account/ChangePassword (or is redirected here by
        the MustChangePassword middleware).
     2. Page renders the form statically (no circuit established).
     3. User enters: current password, new password, confirm new password.
     4. On submit -> EditForm's OnValidSubmit fires in the same static-
        rendered request.
     5. We call IUserAccountService.ChangePasswordAsync(userId, current,
        new). This:
          a. Verifies the current password (returns PasswordMismatch
             error if wrong).
          b. Validates the new password against Identity's complexity
             rules (returns PasswordTooShort / PasswordRequiresDigit /
             etc. if it fails).
          c. Hashes the new password and stores it (rotating the
             SecurityStamp, which invalidates other sessions).
          d. Clears the MustChangePassword flag on the user row.
     6. On success:
          - FORCED flow (user had the `must_change_password` claim):
            Sign the user OUT and redirect to /Account/Login. The
            operator/admin's known password no longer works; the user
            must re-authenticate with the new password they just chose.
            This is the security guarantee: the cookie is freshly minted
            with the user's own password, not the creator's known
            password. No success banner is shown (the login page IS the
            success indicator).
          - VOLUNTARY flow (no `must_change_password` claim):
            Re-issue the auth cookie WITHOUT the must_change_password
            claim (defense-in-depth — the claim shouldn't be there in
            the voluntary flow, but we strip it just in case), then
            IMMEDIATELY redirect to the role-aware landing page (or the
            returnUrl if the user came here from a deep link). No
            success banner is shown — the redirect replaces the entire
            page, so the new landing page IS the success indicator.
     7. On failure, we re-render the form with the Identity error
        message. The alert slot uses a single `_alertKind` enum
        (None / MustChange / Error / Success) so it is IMPOSSIBLE for
        two alerts to render at the same time. This replaces the
        previous three-boolean design which could leak two stacked
        alerts when a code path forgot to clear one before setting
        another — the user perceived this as "two toast messages
        that can't be closed" (RadzenAlert.ShowClose requires a Blazor
        circuit, which doesn't exist in static SSR).

   ANTI-FORGERY:
     EditForm automatically emits an antiforgery token. The
     UseAntiforgery middleware in Program.cs validates it on POST. No
     manual [ValidateAntiForgeryToken] needed. *@

@page "/Account/ChangePassword"
@layout LoginLayout
@using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations
@using System.Security.Claims
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity
@using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging
@using Microsoft.Extensions.Localization
@using TakOne.Application.Common.Authorization
@using TakOne.Application.Common.Interfaces
@using TakOne.Domain.Users
@using TakOne.Infrastructure.Identity
@using TakOne.Infrastructure.Persistence
@using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore
@inject IUserAccountService UserAccountService
@inject SignInManager<ApplicationUser> SignInManager
@inject UserManager<ApplicationUser> UserManager
@inject IHttpContextAccessor HttpContextAccessor
@inject NavigationManager Navigation
@inject ILoggerFactory LoggerFactory
@inject IStringLocalizerFactory LocalizerFactory

<PageTitle>@_loc["PageTitle"]</PageTitle>

@* ── Page-scoped styles for the password row + eye-toggle button ──
   Same .password-row / .password-toggle pattern as CreateUser.razor —
   see that file for the original. The toggle button here is a plain
   <button> (NOT RadzenButton) because this page renders STATICALLY (no
   Blazor circuit) and RadzenButton's Click handler needs a circuit to
   round-trip. A plain <button> with onclick="takTogglePw(this)" calls
   the inline JS function at the bottom of this file — pure browser, no
   server round-trip.

   LAYOUT (revised — eye is INSIDE the textbox, not beside it):
   The .password-row is a positioning context (position:relative, block).
   The RadzenFormField renders the label + input at full width (forced
   via the .password-row .rz-form-field rule below — see comment there).
   The .password-toggle button is position:absolute over the RIGHT edge of
   the input (always the physical right — see `right: 8px` below),
   vertically centered with the input's text. Because absolute
   positioning takes the button OUT of the field's layout flow, the
   input stays at its full width and the eye sits visually INSIDE the
   textbox.

   POSITIONING (eye always on the RIGHT, in both LTR and RTL):
   The <input> inside .password-row is forced to dir="ltr" (see the
   bidi-fix comment above each input element). This means the typed
   text ALWAYS starts from the LEFT edge of the input and extends
   to the RIGHT, regardless of the page's RTL/LTR direction.
   Therefore the eye must ALWAYS sit on the physical RIGHT edge of
   the textbox (where the text ends) — never on the left, where the
   text starts and would collide with the eye.
   We use PHYSICAL `right:8px` + `padding-right:36px` (NOT the CSS
   logical properties `inset-inline-end` / `padding-inline-end`).
   Reason: logical properties flip with the `dir` attribute on <html> —
   in RTL (Persian) they would resolve to `left:8px` / `padding-left:36px`,
   placing the eye on the LEFT — exactly where the (always-LTR) text
   starts, causing the eye to overlap the typed text. Physical `right`
   does NOT flip with `dir`, so the eye stays on the right in both
   English and Persian, matching the English layout the user expects.
   This supersedes the earlier `inset-inline-end` / `padding-inline-end`
   approach, which was correct ONLY when the input itself was RTL
   (i.e. before the dir="ltr" bidi fix was applied to the input). *@
<HeadContent>
    <style>
            .password-row {
                position: relative;
                display: block;
                width: 100%;
            }

            /* Force the RadzenFormField (renders as .rz-form-field with
               display:inline-flex by default) to stretch to 100% width.
               Without this, the field shrinks to its content width when
               placed inside a block-level parent (.password-row), making
               the password textbox visibly narrower than other fields on
               the page. Width:100% on .rz-form-field forces the wrapper
               to fill the .password-row, and the input inside fills the
               wrapper — matching the width of any non-password field. */
            .password-row .rz-form-field {
                width: 100%;
            }

            .password-toggle {
                position: absolute;
                /* RadzenFormField renders label-above + input-below.
                   The input sits at the bottom of the form-field wrapper
                   and is ~40px tall, so the input's vertical center is
                   ~20px from the bottom of the wrapper. The button is
                   32px tall (so its center is 16px from its bottom edge);
                   to align the button's center with the input's center,
                   bottom = 20 - 16 = 4px. */
                bottom: 4px;
                /* Physical `right` (not `inset-inline-end`) — the input
                   is forced dir="ltr" so text always starts from the left;
                   the eye therefore always belongs on the physical right
                   edge of the textbox, in both English and Persian modes.
                   See the big comment block above  */
                right: 8px;
                width: 32px;
                height: 32px;
                padding: 0;
                display: inline-flex;
                align-items: center;
                justify-content: center;
                background: transparent;
                color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color, #6b7280);
                border: none;
                border-radius: var(--rz-border-radius, 6px);
                cursor: pointer;
                transition: background-color 0.15s ease, color 0.15s ease;
                /* Ensure the button sits above the input (so the click
                   target is on top, not the input). */
                z-index: 1;
            }

                .password-toggle:hover {
                    background: var(--rz-base-hover-bg, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.06));
                    color: var(--rz-text-color, #111827);
                }

                .password-toggle:focus-visible {
                    outline: 2px solid var(--rz-primary, #3b82f6);
                    outline-offset: 2px;
                }

                .password-toggle svg {
                    pointer-events: none;
                }

            /* Reserve space INSIDE the input on the RIGHT so the typed
               text doesn't run under the absolutely-positioned eye icon.
               Uses PHYSICAL `padding-right` (NOT the CSS logical property
               `padding-inline-end`) — the input is forced dir="ltr" so
               text always starts from the left and extends to the right,
               and the eye always sits on the physical right. !important
               is needed to beat Radzen's default .rz-textbox padding rule.
               Applies to any <input> inside .password-row (RadzenPassword
               renders as input.rz-textbox, and our native <input> uses
               class="rz-textbox" too). */
            .password-row input {
                padding-right: 36px !important;
            }
    </style>
</HeadContent>

<RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Vertical" Gap="1rem" Style="max-width: 360px; margin: 0 auto;">
    @* --- Single alert slot. Uses a single _alertKind enum so it is
           IMPOSSIBLE for two alerts to render at the same time.

           PREVIOUS DESIGN (Issue #08): three independent booleans
           (_showMustChangeBanner, _showSuccess) plus a string
           (_errorMessage). Multiple booleans could be true
           simultaneously if a code path forgot to clear one before
           setting another, and the result was TWO stacked alerts that
           the user couldn't close (RadzenAlert.ShowClose requires a
           Blazor circuit, which doesn't exist in static SSR). The
           single-enum design makes mutual exclusion a compile-time
           guarantee rather than a discipline-the-developer convention.

           NOTE on ShowClose: deliberately NOT set. RadzenAlert's close
           button needs a Blazor circuit to handle the click, and this
           page renders statically. The button would render but be inert.
           Instead, the alert is automatically cleared on the next form
           submission (see `_alertKind = AlertKind.None;` at the top of
           HandleChangePasswordAsync). --- *@
    @if (_alertKind == AlertKind.MustChange)
    {
        <RadzenAlert Title="@_loc["MustChangeTitle"]"
                     Text="@_loc["MustChangeMessage"]"
                     AlertStyle="AlertStyle.Warning"
                     Variant="Variant.Filled"
                     ShowIcon="true" />
    }
    else if (_alertKind == AlertKind.Error)
    {
        <RadzenAlert Title="@_loc["ErrorTitle"]"
                     Text="@_alertMessage"
                     AlertStyle="AlertStyle.Danger"
                     Variant="Variant.Filled"
                     ShowIcon="true" />
    }
    else if (_alertKind == AlertKind.Success)
    {
        <RadzenAlert Title="@_loc["SuccessTitle"]"
                     Text="@_loc["SuccessMessage"]"
                     AlertStyle="AlertStyle.Success"
                     Variant="Variant.Filled"
                     ShowIcon="true" />
    }

    <EditForm Model="@FormModel"
              OnValidSubmit="@HandleChangePasswordAsync"
              FormName="changePasswordForm"
              Method="post"
              Enhance="false">
        @* ↑ Enhance="false" for the same reason as Login.razor --- the
           browser does a normal full-page POST so the antiforgery token
           is consumed cleanly and the re-issued cookie (without the
           must_change_password claim) takes effect on the redirect
           response. *@

        <DataAnnotationsValidator />

        <RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Vertical" Gap="0.75rem">
            @* Each password field is wrapped in .password-row (a relative
               positioning context). The RadzenFormField renders the label
               + input at full natural width, and the plain <button>
               eye-toggle is absolutely positioned over the right edge of
               the input (visually INSIDE the textbox, not beside it). The
               button is a plain HTML <button> — NOT a RadzenButton —
               because RadzenButton's Click handler needs a Blazor circuit,
               and this page is static-SSR (see the big comment at the top
               of the file). The button's onclick attribute calls
               takTogglePw(this), a tiny inline JS function at the bottom
               of this file.

               Two inline SVGs are stacked inside the button:
                 .pw-icon-eye     → shown when password is MASKED (initial)
                 .pw-icon-eye-off → shown when password is VISIBLE
               JS toggles their display style + the input's type attribute.

               data-show-label / data-hide-label carry the localized strings
               so the JS can swap the title + aria-label without a server
               round-trip. They're read from _loc (the page's localizer)
               on the server at render time, so they respect the current
               culture (fa-IR / en-US). *@
            <div class="password-row">
                <RadzenFormField Text="@_loc["CurrentPasswordLabel"]">
                    @* Native <input type="password" dir="ltr"> instead of
                       RadzenPassword. WHY: In RTL (fa-IR) mode, the bidi
                       algorithm treats weak/neutral characters like "!" as
                       RTL-directional, placing them at the START of the LTR
                       run (the right edge in RTL). The user types
                       "miow2323!" but sees "!miow2323". Setting dir="ltr"
                       on the input forces the browser to treat the entire
                       input as LTR, preserving the typed character order.
                       Same fix as Login.razor, CreateProduct.razor, and
                       ProductDetail.razor. The eye-toggle JS (takTogglePw)
                       still works — it finds the input via
                       row.querySelector('input'), matching a native <input>
                       the same as RadzenPassword's rendered <input>. The
                       name attribute is REQUIRED for [SupplyParameterFromForm]. *@
                    <input type="password"
                           class="rz-textbox"
                           dir="ltr"
                           name="FormModel.CurrentPassword"
                           placeholder="@_loc["CurrentPasswordPlaceholder"]"
                           autocomplete="current-password"
                           autofocus
                           @bind="FormModel!.CurrentPassword"
                           style="width: 100%; text-align: left;" />
                </RadzenFormField>
                <button type="button"
                        class="password-toggle"
                        title="@_loc["ShowPassword"]"
                        aria-label="@_loc["ShowPassword"]"
                        aria-pressed="false"
                        data-show-label="@_loc["ShowPassword"]"
                        data-hide-label="@_loc["HidePassword"]"
                        onclick="takTogglePw(this)">
                    <svg class="pw-icon-eye" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20"
                         fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"
                         stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
                        <path d="M2 12s3-7 10-7 10 7 10 7-3 7-10 7-10-7-10-7z"></path>
                        <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3"></circle>
                    </svg>
                    <svg class="pw-icon-eye-off" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20"
                         fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"
                         stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"
                         style="display: none;">
                        <path d="M17.94 17.94A10.07 10.07 0 0 1 12 20c-7 0-10-8-10-8a18.45 18.45 0 0 1 5.06-5.94M9.9 4.24A9.12 9.12 0 0 1 12 4c7 0 10 8 10 8a18.5 18.5 0 0 1-2.16 3.19m-6.72-1.07a3 3 0 1 1-4.24-4.24"></path>
                        <line x1="1" y1="1" x2="23" y2="23"></line>
                    </svg>
                </button>
            </div>

            <div class="password-row">
                <RadzenFormField Text="@_loc["NewPasswordLabel"]">
                    @* Native <input type="password" dir="ltr"> — same RTL
                       bidi fix as CurrentPassword above. See that comment
                       for the full explanation. *@
                    <input type="password"
                           class="rz-textbox"
                           dir="ltr"
                           name="FormModel.NewPassword"
                           placeholder="@_loc["NewPasswordPlaceholder"]"
                           autocomplete="new-password"
                           @bind="FormModel!.NewPassword"
                           style="width: 100%; text-align: left;" />
                </RadzenFormField>
                <button type="button"
                        class="password-toggle"
                        title="@_loc["ShowPassword"]"
                        aria-label="@_loc["ShowPassword"]"
                        aria-pressed="false"
                        data-show-label="@_loc["ShowPassword"]"
                        data-hide-label="@_loc["HidePassword"]"
                        onclick="takTogglePw(this)">
                    <svg class="pw-icon-eye" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20"
                         fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"
                         stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
                        <path d="M2 12s3-7 10-7 10 7 10 7-3 7-10 7-10-7-10-7z"></path>
                        <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3"></circle>
                    </svg>
                    <svg class="pw-icon-eye-off" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20"
                         fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"
                         stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"
                         style="display: none;">
                        <path d="M17.94 17.94A10.07 10.07 0 0 1 12 20c-7 0-10-8-10-8a18.45 18.45 0 0 1 5.06-5.94M9.9 4.24A9.12 9.12 0 0 1 12 4c7 0 10 8 10 8a18.5 18.5 0 0 1-2.16 3.19m-6.72-1.07a3 3 0 1 1-4.24-4.24"></path>
                        <line x1="1" y1="1" x2="23" y2="23"></line>
                    </svg>
                </button>
            </div>

            <div class="password-row">
                <RadzenFormField Text="@_loc["ConfirmPasswordLabel"]">
                    @* Native <input type="password" dir="ltr"> — same RTL
                       bidi fix as CurrentPassword above. See that comment
                       for the full explanation. *@
                    <input type="password"
                           class="rz-textbox"
                           dir="ltr"
                           name="FormModel.ConfirmPassword"
                           placeholder="@_loc["ConfirmPasswordPlaceholder"]"
                           autocomplete="new-password"
                           @bind="FormModel!.ConfirmPassword"
                           style="width: 100%; text-align: left;" />
                </RadzenFormField>
                <button type="button"
                        class="password-toggle"
                        title="@_loc["ShowPassword"]"
                        aria-label="@_loc["ShowPassword"]"
                        aria-pressed="false"
                        data-show-label="@_loc["ShowPassword"]"
                        data-hide-label="@_loc["HidePassword"]"
                        onclick="takTogglePw(this)">
                    <svg class="pw-icon-eye" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20"
                         fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"
                         stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
                        <path d="M2 12s3-7 10-7 10 7 10 7-3 7-10 7-10-7-10-7z"></path>
                        <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3"></circle>
                    </svg>
                    <svg class="pw-icon-eye-off" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20"
                         fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"
                         stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"
                         style="display: none;">
                        <path d="M17.94 17.94A10.07 10.07 0 0 1 12 20c-7 0-10-8-10-8a18.45 18.45 0 0 1 5.06-5.94M9.9 4.24A9.12 9.12 0 0 1 12 4c7 0 10 8 10 8a18.5 18.5 0 0 1-2.16 3.19m-6.72-1.07a3 3 0 1 1-4.24-4.24"></path>
                        <line x1="1" y1="1" x2="23" y2="23"></line>
                    </svg>
                </button>
            </div>

            @* Password complexity hint --- helps the user meet the
               Identity complexity rules configured in appsettings.json
               (8+ chars, upper + lower + digit + non-alphanumeric). *@
            <RadzenText Text="@_loc["PasswordRequirements"]"
                        Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); font-size: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.4;" />

            <RadzenButton Text="@_loc["SubmitButton"]"
                          ButtonType="ButtonType.Submit"
                          ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Primary"
                          Variant="Variant.Filled"
                          Icon="lock_reset"
                          Disabled="@_isSubmitting"
                          Style="width: 100%;" />
        </RadzenStack>
    </EditForm>
</RadzenStack>

@* ── Inline toggle script ──
   WHY INLINE (not a separate .js file):
     - The function is 5 lines of trivial DOM manipulation — too small
       to justify a separate file, an extra HTTP request, a registration
       in App.razor, and a cache-bust version parameter.
     - It's page-local: the function name `takTogglePw` is unique to
       this page, so there's no reuse argument for hoisting it into a
       shared file.
     - It's a global function declaration (no IIFE, no DOM access at
       parse time), so it's safe to inline — the browser registers
       window.takTogglePw when the <script> tag is parsed, and the
       onclick="takTogglePw(this)" attribute fires it on user click,
       well after the DOM is fully built.

   WHY NOT a Blazor @onclick handler:
     - This page is STATIC SSR (see the top-of-file comment). Blazor's
       @onclick requires a circuit to round-trip the click event to the
       server. With no circuit, the handler silently never fires. The
       onclick HTML attribute, by contrast, is the browser's native
       click handler — no circuit needed.

   WHAT IT DOES:
     1. Finds the .password-row wrapper containing the clicked button.
     2. Finds the <input> inside that wrapper (the password field).
     3. Toggles the input's `type` attribute between "password" and "text".
     4. Swaps which of the two SVGs (.pw-icon-eye / .pw-icon-eye-off)
        is visible by toggling their `display` style.
     5. Updates the button's title, aria-label, and aria-pressed to
        reflect the new state. The localized strings are read from the
        button's data-show-label / data-hide-label attributes (which
        were populated by _loc at render time, so they respect the
        current UI culture).
   *@
<script>
    function takTogglePw(btn) {
        var row = btn.closest('.password-row');
        if (!row) return;
        var input = row.querySelector('input');
        if (!input) return;
        var eyeOn = btn.querySelector('.pw-icon-eye');
        var eyeOff = btn.querySelector('.pw-icon-eye-off');
        var showLabel = btn.getAttribute('data-show-label') || 'Show password';
        var hideLabel = btn.getAttribute('data-hide-label') || 'Hide password';
        if (input.type === 'password') {
            input.type = 'text';
            if (eyeOn) eyeOn.style.display = 'none';
            if (eyeOff) eyeOff.style.display = '';
            btn.setAttribute('title', hideLabel);
            btn.setAttribute('aria-label', hideLabel);
            btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', 'true');
        } else {
            input.type = 'password';
            if (eyeOn) eyeOn.style.display = '';
            if (eyeOff) eyeOff.style.display = 'none';
            btn.setAttribute('title', showLabel);
            btn.setAttribute('aria-label', showLabel);
            btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', 'false');
        }
    }
</script>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Form model for the change password form.
    ///
    /// CurrentPassword is verified by UserManager.ChangePasswordAsync
    /// (which the IUserAccountService.ChangePasswordAsync implementation
    /// calls). NewPassword is validated against Identity's complexity
    /// rules (also by UserManager.ChangePasswordAsync). ConfirmPassword
    /// uses [Compare] to enforce equality with NewPassword --- Blazor's
    /// validation surfaces the comparison error automatically.
    /// </summary>
    private sealed class ChangePasswordModel
    {
        [Required(ErrorMessage = "Current password is required.")]
        public string CurrentPassword { get; set; } = string.Empty;

        [Required(ErrorMessage = "New password is required.")]
        [StringLength(100, MinimumLength = 8, ErrorMessage = "Password must be at least 8 characters long.")]
        public string NewPassword { get; set; } = string.Empty;

        [Required(ErrorMessage = "Please confirm your new password.")]
        [Compare(nameof(NewPassword), ErrorMessage = "The passwords do not match.")]
        public string ConfirmPassword { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    }

    [SupplyParameterFromForm(FormName = "changePasswordForm")]
    private ChangePasswordModel? FormModel { get; set; }

    // Logger + localizer created via the *Factory abstractions rather than
    // ILogger<ChangePassword>/IStringLocalizer<ChangePassword>. Same reason
    // as ResetPassword.razor --- sidesteps a Razor source-generator quirk
    // on .NET 10 with [SupplyParameterFromForm]. See ResetPassword.razor
    // for the full explanation.
    //
    // CRITICAL — baseName must be the FULL namespace-qualified resource name
    // (not just "ChangePassword"). The Razor SDK embeds resx files using
    // their full path relative to the project root, so the resource stream
    // is named `TakOne.WebUI.Components.Pages.Account.ChangePassword.
    // ChangePassword.fa-IR.resources` (note the folder path + the .resx
    // filename repeated). ResourceManager uses the baseName LITERALLY ---
    // `new ResourceManager("ChangePassword", asm)` looks for a stream named
    // `ChangePassword.fa-IR.resources`, which doesn't exist, so the
    // localizer silently falls back to returning the raw key (and the page
    // shows "CurrentPasswordLabel", "SubmitButton", etc. as literal text).
    // The full path matches the embedded resource name and the localizer
    // finds the translations. `IStringLocalizer<T>` does this automatically
    // via typeof(T).FullName --- we just have to do it manually here.
    private ILogger _logger = default!;
    private IStringLocalizer _loc = default!;

    /// <summary>
    /// Single-source-of-truth for which alert (if any) is visible.
    /// Replaces the previous three-boolean design (which allowed two
    /// alerts to be visible at once when a code path forgot to clear
    /// one before setting another — see markup comment above).
    /// </summary>
    private enum AlertKind
    {
        None,
        MustChange,
        Error,
        Success
    }

    private AlertKind _alertKind = AlertKind.None;
    private string _alertMessage = string.Empty;
    private bool _isSubmitting;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        _logger = LoggerFactory.CreateLogger("ChangePassword");
        // Full namespace-qualified baseName — see comment above the field
        // declaration for why this can't be just "ChangePassword".
        _loc = LocalizerFactory.Create(
            "TakOne.WebUI.Components.Pages.Account.ChangePassword.ChangePassword",
            "TakOne.WebUI");
        FormModel ??= new ChangePasswordModel();

        var httpContext = HttpContextAccessor.HttpContext;

        // --------------------------------------------------------------
        // If the user is NOT authenticated, they have no business on
        // this page --- redirect to login. (They might have hit /Account/
        // ChangePassword directly in the URL bar without signing in.)
        // --------------------------------------------------------------
        if (httpContext?.User?.Identity?.IsAuthenticated != true)
        {
            Navigation.NavigateTo("/Account/Login", forceLoad: true);
            return;
        }

        // --------------------------------------------------------------
        // If the user has the `must_change_password` claim, this is a
        // forced-first-login flow --- show the warning banner so they
        // understand why they're here instead of on the dashboard.
        // --------------------------------------------------------------
        if (httpContext!.User.HasClaim("must_change_password", "true"))
        {
            _alertKind = AlertKind.MustChange;
        }
    }

    private async Task HandleChangePasswordAsync()
    {
        var model = FormModel!;
        _isSubmitting = true;
        // Clear any existing alert so a fresh submit doesn't keep a stale
        // banner visible alongside a new error (Issue #08 — duplicate
        // alerts). With the single-enum design this is a single assignment.
        _alertKind = AlertKind.None;
        _alertMessage = string.Empty;

        try
        {
            var httpContext = HttpContextAccessor.HttpContext;
            if (httpContext is null)
            {
                _alertKind = AlertKind.Error;
                _alertMessage = _loc["Error_Unexpected"];
                return;
            }

            // ------------------------------------------------------------
            // Determine which flow we're in: FORCED (the user was
            // redirected here by the MustChangePassword middleware
            // because their cookie carries the `must_change_password`
            // claim) or VOLUNTARY (the user navigated here directly
            // to change their own password).
            //
            // The two flows have DIFFERENT success behavior:
            //   - FORCED:   sign the user OUT and redirect to /Account/Login.
            //   - VOLUNTARY: re-issue the cookie and redirect to the
            //                role-aware landing page (or returnUrl).
            //
            // We read the claim HERE (not in OnInitialized) because the
            // claim's presence is the canonical signal — and reading it
            // here keeps the decision local to the success path where
            // it's used.
            // ------------------------------------------------------------
            var isForcedChange = httpContext.User.HasClaim("must_change_password", "true");

            // ------------------------------------------------------------
            // Defense-in-depth: reject the new password if it's identical
            // to the current password. Identity's UserManager.ChangePasswordAsync
            // does NOT enforce this by default (it has no password-history
            // feature), so we enforce it here. This is especially
            // important for the forced-first-login flow --- the whole
            // point of forcing a change is to ensure the new password is
            // DIFFERENT from the one the operator configured.
            // ------------------------------------------------------------
            if (string.Equals(model.CurrentPassword, model.NewPassword,
                StringComparison.Ordinal))
            {
                _alertKind = AlertKind.Error;
                _alertMessage = _loc["Error_NewPasswordSameAsCurrent"];
                return;
            }

            // ------------------------------------------------------------
            // Resolve the current user's Id from the auth cookie's
            // NameIdentifier claim. We don't trust UserName here --- the
            // user might have just had their UserName changed by an
            // admin (rare but possible), and the cookie might be stale.
            // The NameIdentifier claim IS the user's immutable Guid Id
            // and is the canonical way to look up the ApplicationUser.
            // ------------------------------------------------------------
            var userIdClaim = httpContext.User.FindFirst(ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier)?.Value;
            if (userIdClaim is null || !Guid.TryParse(userIdClaim, out var userId))
            {
                _logger.LogError(
                    "ChangePassword: authenticated user has no parseable " +
                    "NameIdentifier claim. Claim value: '{ClaimValue}'.",
                    userIdClaim ?? "(null)");
                _alertKind = AlertKind.Error;
                _alertMessage = _loc["Error_Unexpected"];
                return;
            }

            // ------------------------------------------------------------
            // Call the Application-layer abstraction. The implementation
            // (UserAccountService) verifies the current password, validates
            // the new password, hashes + stores it, rotates the
            // SecurityStamp, and clears the MustChangePassword flag ---
            // all in one atomic call.
            // ------------------------------------------------------------
            var result = await UserAccountService.ChangePasswordAsync(
                userId,
                model.CurrentPassword,
                model.NewPassword);

            if (!result.IsSuccess)
            {
                // Identity's error message --- already localized by the
                // IdentityErrorDescriber. For wrong current password,
                // it'll say "Incorrect password." For complexity
                // failures, it'll say "Passwords must have at least one
                // digit ('0'-'9')." etc.
                _alertKind = AlertKind.Error;
                _alertMessage = result.Error;
                _logger.LogWarning(
                    "ChangePassword: ChangePasswordAsync failed for userId {UserId}. Error: {Error}.",
                    userId, _alertMessage);
                return;
            }

            _logger.LogInformation(
                "ChangePassword: password changed for userId {UserId} (forced flow: {IsForced}).",
                userId, isForcedChange);

            // ============================================================
            // FORCED-FIRST-LOGIN FLOW
            // ============================================================
            // The user was redirected here by the MustChangePassword
            // middleware because their cookie carried the
            // `must_change_password` claim. The password has now been
            // changed and the MustChangePassword flag on the user row
            // has been cleared (by UserAccountService.ChangePasswordAsync).
            //
            // SECURITY GUARANTEE:
            //   We deliberately do NOT re-issue the cookie here. Instead,
            //   we SIGN THE USER OUT and redirect to /Account/Login.
            //   Why: the cookie the user is currently holding was minted
            //   from a sign-in with a password that was set by SOMEONE
            //   ELSE (the operator, an admin, or a manager). Even though
            //   the password has been changed on the user row, the
            //   cookie's authentication is still anchored to the OLD
            //   sign-in. The cleanest way to guarantee the cookie is
            //   freshly minted from the user's OWN new password is to
            //   force a fresh sign-in.
            //
            //   This also matches the operational guarantee from
            //   DefaultAdminOptions.ForcePasswordChangeOnFirstLogin:
            //   "the operator cannot log in as the admin after first
            //   login" — and the operator cannot log in with the new
            //   password either, because they don't know it.
            //
            // No success banner is shown: the login page itself is the
            // success indicator. The user will see the familiar login
            // form and understand they need to enter their NEW password.
            // ============================================================
            if (isForcedChange)
            {
                await SignInManager.SignOutAsync();
                _logger.LogInformation(
                    "ChangePassword: forced-change flow complete for userId {UserId}. " +
                    "Signed out. Redirecting to /Account/Login.",
                    userId);
                // NavigateTo in static SSR issues a 302 on the CURRENT
                // HTTP response — this works because we haven't returned
                // from the handler yet. The browser follows the redirect
                // and replaces the entire page with the login page. No
                // success banner is rendered.
                Navigation.NavigateTo("/Account/Login", forceLoad: true);
                return;
            }

            // ============================================================
            // VOLUNTARY FLOW (user navigated here directly)
            // ============================================================
            // Re-issue the auth cookie WITHOUT the `must_change_password`
            // claim. In the voluntary flow the claim shouldn't be present
            // anyway, but we strip it defense-in-depth — if a stale
            // claim somehow lingered on the cookie, the middleware would
            // keep redirecting the user here even though they've already
            // changed their password.
            //
            // We re-use the existing custom claims (FullName, GroupName,
            // Gender) by reading them from the current principal --- that
            // avoids a DB lookup just to re-populate them.
            // ============================================================
            var appUser = await UserManager.FindByIdAsync(userId.ToString());
            if (appUser is null)
            {
                // Defensive --- we just changed this user's password, so
                // the row definitely exists. If we somehow can't find
                // it, sign the user out and send them to login.
                _logger.LogError(
                    "ChangePassword: ApplicationUser not found after " +
                    "successful password change for userId {UserId}. " +
                    "Signing the user out.",
                    userId);
                await SignInManager.SignOutAsync();
                Navigation.NavigateTo("/Account/Login", forceLoad: true);
                return;
            }

            var refreshedClaims = new List<Claim>();
            // Copy over the existing custom claims (FullName, GroupName,
            // Gender) so the new cookie has the same enrichment.
            foreach (var claimType in new[] { "FullName", "GroupName", "Gender" })
            {
                var existing = httpContext.User.FindFirst(claimType);
                if (existing is not null)
                {
                    refreshedClaims.Add(existing);
                }
            }
            // Deliberately DO NOT add the `must_change_password` claim
            // here --- the whole point of re-issuing the cookie is to
            // ensure that claim is absent.

            await SignInManager.SignInWithClaimsAsync(
                appUser,
                new AuthenticationProperties { IsPersistent = false },
                refreshedClaims);

            // ------------------------------------------------------------
            // Redirect IMMEDIATELY (no success banner, no Task.Delay).
            //
            // PREVIOUS DESIGN: set _showSuccess = true, then fire-and-forget
            //   _ = Task.Delay(1500).ContinueWith(_ => Navigation.NavigateTo(...));
            // That pattern is BROKEN in static SSR — by the time the
            // Task.Delay completes, the HTTP response has already been
            // sent to the browser (with the success banner rendered).
            // Navigation.NavigateTo can't redirect a response that's
            // already been sent, so the redirect never fires and the
            // success banner stays on screen indefinitely — which the
            // user perceives as an "unclosable toast".
            //
            // NEW DESIGN: redirect synchronously inside the handler.
            // Navigation.NavigateTo in static SSR issues a 302 on the
            // CURRENT HTTP response — the browser follows the redirect
            // and replaces the entire page with the role-aware landing
            // page. No success banner is rendered (the new page is the
            // success indicator). The user is never left staring at a
            // stuck toast.
            // ------------------------------------------------------------
            var returnUrl = httpContext.Request.Query["returnUrl"].ToString();
            var target = !string.IsNullOrEmpty(returnUrl) && IsSafeReturnUrl(returnUrl)
                ? returnUrl
                : GetRoleAwareLandingUrl(httpContext.User);

            _logger.LogInformation(
                "ChangePassword: redirecting to '{Target}' after successful voluntary password change.",
                target);

            Navigation.NavigateTo(target, forceLoad: true);
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _logger.LogError(ex,
                "ChangePassword: unexpected error changing password.");
            _alertKind = AlertKind.Error;
            _alertMessage = _loc["Error_Unexpected"];
        }
        finally
        {
            _isSubmitting = false;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Resolves the role-aware landing URL for the given principal.
    /// Mirrors the same logic in Login.razor --- kept private here so
    /// the two pages don't need a shared helper. (Customer -> /Products;
    /// everyone else -> /.)
    /// </summary>
    private static string GetRoleAwareLandingUrl(ClaimsPrincipal principal)
    {
        if (principal.IsInRole(Roles.Customer))
        {
            return "/Products";
        }
        return "/";
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Open-redirect defense --- mirrors Login.razor's IsSafeReturnUrl.
    /// Only allows relative URLs that don't start with // (protocol-relative)
    /// and don't point at /Account/Login or /Account/ChangePassword itself
    /// (would loop).
    /// </summary>
    private static bool IsSafeReturnUrl(string returnUrl)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(returnUrl)) return false;
        if (!returnUrl.StartsWith('/')) return false;
        if (returnUrl.StartsWith("//")) return false;
        if (returnUrl.StartsWith("/\\", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)) return false;
        if (returnUrl.StartsWith("/Account/Login", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)) return false;
        if (returnUrl.StartsWith("/Account/ChangePassword", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)) return false;
        return true;
    }
}